A||||5||Uaana||Front spring removal?||||1||
Z||000000||Uaana||05-31-2001||06:27 PM||||I've searched. no luck.<BR>So how do you take these things out?<BR>how many other parts will I need to replace along the way? Special tools other than spring compressor?||||reg||1
Z||000001||coyote||05-31-2001||06:53 PM||||I did not use a spring compressor. I just wrapped a chain around the spring just in case.<P>Take the tire off, support the car. then slide the jack under the lower arm and compress the spring. remove the brakes,rotor,tie rod end,sway bar and shock.<BR>remove the cotter pins and castle nuts from the spindel. Pop the joints loose with a pickel fork. Lower the jack slowly and pull the spindle out.  let the jack down and yanl the spring out. mine were in there pretty good and took a little coaxing<P>coyote||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierosound||05-31-2001||08:36 PM||||There is a Fiero on-line service guide.  Very handy for those who haven't yet bought a Fiero manual (Haynes or Helms).<P>To remove front springs, go here:   <A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/front-springs.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/front-springs.html</A> <P>||||reg||10
Z||000003||Bob Englert||05-31-2001||09:20 PM||||I had all kinds of problems removing and particularly reinstalling the<BR>springs in my 85 SE.  I could not compress the springs enough to get the<BR>springs seated.  They are VERY stiff and are under a lot of pressure when<BR>you try to reseat them, particularly if you are planning to reinstall the<BR>stock springs.  Lowering springs will be much easier to install and probably<BR>will not need the method I discuss below.<P>What I describe here has worked well for me on several cars since then.  I<BR>use a coil spring lowering kit from JC Whitney that is supposed to be used<BR>to pull the springs down while installed on the car.  While I would never<BR>use them for this purpose, they work very well to hold the springs<BR>compressed enough to get then out and back in the car.<P>The part can be found at <A HREF="http://www.jcwhitney.com/SearchCatContainer.jhtml?_requestid=30690" TARGET=_blank>http://www.jcwhitney.com/SearchCatContainer.jhtml?_requestid=30690</A> <P>After separating the upper and lower ball joints from the spindle, I<BR>installed the clamps to hold the spring front and back as you face the<BR>spring.  I then raise the jack until the car is just lifting off the jack<BR>stand (max compression of the spring) and tighten the clamps in the kit.  I<BR>then lowered the jack all the way to take pressure off the spring.  I should<BR>be fairly easy to remove at this point.<P>I would strongly recommend a Haynes manual for the steps in the suspension<BR>disassembly.  This would be a good time to replace the bushings and ball<BR>joints while things are apart.<P><P>------------------<BR>Bob<BR>2 - 87 GT's<BR>85 SE 4 cyl||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||06-01-2001||12:37 AM||||I found that it was most convenient to tie the spring down while it was installed in the suspension,using ten guage,non-galvanized wire.I made about four passes just to be safe.Weighing down the car with an extra body helps too,while you're pulling and twisting wire.<BR>Since it's pretty tough to use any type of compressor in the vehicle,that's the best I came up with after some ten or so spring swaps.The springs can be compressed out of the car,and tied down for installation.||||reg||1
Z||000005||terryk||06-01-2001||12:55 AM||||To remove it, I loosen the lower control arm bolts, raise the car so the lower control arm can go almost vertical and not hit the ground, put a jack under the arm, lossen the nut, pop the ball joint, remove the ball joint nut and lower the arm. Then I place a rope around the lower part of the spring and pull. The spring will pop out.<P>To install it, I use one foot to hold the arm vertical, then kick the lower part of the spring onto the perch. I use my foot to hold it there and then jack up the control arm.<P>Even if you slip, the spring doesn't head for the next county, it just pops back out.<P>What you don't want to have is the jack coming out before the lower ball joint nut is in place once you start jacking the arm up. In that case, it could head for Texas.||||reg||1
